## Note
This is an instance of **adjunct attachment ambiguity**, not argument structure ambiguity. The complement structure of **tell** remains the same in both parses; only the position/scope of the adverb differs.

If you want, I can also provide:
- a **more explicitly X-bar-compliant version** with intermediate projections for every category,
- a version using **CP/TP shells** more standard in current syntax,
- or **bracketed structures** instead of `forest` code.

Yes. The sentence is syntactically ambiguous because the coordinator **and** can attach at two different levels.

Sentence:
> **Francisco believed his peers were idiots and they resented him.**

## Two main parses

### 1. Coordination of two embedded clauses
Meaning:
- Francisco believed **[his peers were idiots]**
- and Francisco believed **[they resented him]**

So **and** joins two clauses inside the complement of **believed**.

Structure:
\[
\text{Francisco } [\text{believed } [\text{his peers were idiots}] \text{ and } [\text{they resented him}]]
\]

### 2. Coordination of two matrix clauses
Meaning:
- **Francisco believed [his peers were idiots]**
- and **they resented him**

So **and** joins two full clauses at the top level.

Structure:
\[
[\text{Francisco believed [his peers were idiots]}] \text{ and } [\text{they resented him}]
\]

---

