3. **Expanded recursive version**

Add one more possessive layer of the same type:

**The Old Continent's civilizations' scholars' advances have shaped history.**

Now the possessive chain is:

\[
[[[[\text{the Old Continent}]'s \ \text{civilizations}]' \ \text{scholars}]' \ \text{advances}]
\]

This adds another layer of **possessive recursion**.

1. **Recursion?**  
Yes. The sentence contains **prepositional phrase (PP) recursion** inside the noun phrase:

- **their recipe [for the basic dough [of the pie crust]]**

Here, the NP **the basic dough** is modified by the PP **of the pie crust**, and that larger NP is itself the complement of another PP **for the basic dough of the pie crust**. So this is **PP embedding / PP recursion**.

A simplified bracketed view:

- \[
[N\!P\ \text{their recipe}\ [P\!P\ \text{for}\ [N\!P\ \text{the basic dough}\ [P\!P\ \text{of}\ [N\!P\ \text{the pie crust}]]]]]
\]

